{
  "dom_id": "step-q335064",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "A compact geological sensor of mass $6\\,\\text{kg}$ is launched vertically downward with an initial velocity of $1.8\\,\\text{m/s}.$ Let $v(t)$ be the sensor's velocity, in meters per second, $t\\ge 0$ seconds after release, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance acting on the sensor is negligible, and the only other force acting on it is its weight due to gravity.\n\nWhat is the velocity of the sensor after $3$ seconds? Assume that acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nThe velocity after $3$ seconds is\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m/s}.$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall Newton's second law, which states that the resultant force $F$ acting on a body equals the product of its mass $m$ and acceleration $a:$\n\n$$\nF=ma\n$$\n\nSince air resistance is negligible, the only force acting on the sensor is its weight due to gravity. The weight $w$ of the sensor is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =w \\\\  & =mg \\\\  & =6g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that acceleration is the derivative of velocity: $a=\\frac{dv}{dt}.$  Substituting this into Newton's second law, along with our expression for the resultant force on the sensor, we get\n\n$$\n6g=6\\,\\frac{dv}{dt}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying and writing in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{dv}{dt}=g.\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate to solve for $v(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{dv}{dt}\\,dt=g\\int dt\n$$\n\n$$\nv(t)=gt+C\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration. We're told that the initial velocity of the sensor is $1.8\\,\\text{m/s}.$ So, we apply the initial condition $v(0)=1.8$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\nv(0)=g\\cdot 0+C\n$$\n\n$$\n1.8=0+C\n$$\n\n$$\nC=1.8\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity of the sensor $t$ seconds after release is\n\n$$\nv(t)=gt+1.8.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity after $3$ seconds is\n\n$$\nv(3)=9.8\\cdot 3+1.8=31.2\\,\\text{m/s}.\n$$",
